A||||8||Master_Sushi||Adjusting Parking Brake||||3||
Z||000000||Master_Sushi||04-27-2001||07:40 PM||||Well, My parking brake has had allot of slack for a while now (I could pull it up all the way and it didn't grab much) and I've been meaning to adjust it for a while. I just finally jacked up my 88coupe tonight and tightened up the adjusting bolt. After I did this the parking brake is much tighter and stronger. The odd thing is, my brakes all together feel much firmer and stronger. My brake pedal travel is less then an 1/8 inch now. I know before I adjusted the parking brake it was probably more then 1/2 an inch. I originally thought I tightened it to much (I turned the nut like 5 times) but it seems ok. I cant feel the rears dragging at all, and I've been up to highway speeds and my rear tires didn't melt  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. Did a few brake test and seemed fine, can still lock all 4 wheels up. Were my brakes just unadjusted? Or did I screw them up? I know I just had my brakes done a few months ago, I would think they would check this...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000001||Phil||04-27-2001||08:07 PM||||Quite normal- regular use of the hand brake keeps the rear calipers adjusted||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||04-27-2001||09:58 PM||||You can make it tighter by loosening the lock nut on cable under cradle by left rear wheel and tightening the adjusting nut. Then make sure you tighten lock nut back.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||04-27-2001||11:01 PM||||Make sure you didn't over tighten the cables... you want the arms to return to the stops or bloody close to it.<P>Rear disk cars all, at least all I know of, require regular use of the hand brake to work right. Some are less picky than others.<P>It's really hard to explain how these calipers work. I am working on finding decent cross sections so people can see how they work. They are amazing little monsters....||||reg||1
Z||000004||ka4nkf||04-28-2001||12:38 AM||||After you get the slack out of the cable and it still rest on the stop, Then GM recommends that you pull your E-brake lever all the way, then push the brake pedal down pretty hard and release the E-brake lever, then release your foot off the brake pedal. you may have to repeat this process 5 or 6 times to get the piston and E-brake lever synchronized. When you use your E-brake always put your foot on the brake pedal before you pull the E-brake and always put your foot on the brake pedal when you release the E_brake. Doing this they will stay adjusted.Like ogre said they are amazing. BTW I don't think you can do this on the 84 I think it has different pistons and you will have to install the newer caliper kit. hope this makes sense||||reg||1
Z||000005||ka4nkf||04-28-2001||01:37 AM||||In my post I stated that you put your foot on the brake pedal before you pull the E-brake lever and always put your foot on the brake pedal when you release the E-brake lever. This is not right. Do it the following way.<P>Do not apply pressure on the brake pedal when you pull the E-brake lever. Only apply pressure on foot pedal when you release the E-brake lever. When you apply pressure on the brake pedal to release the E-brake, this pressure holds the piston and keeps it from turning when you release the E-brake therefore keeping the E-brake adjusted. This is really hard to explain.But just think about it and you will get the picture.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||04-28-2001||11:58 AM||||Ummm..... The piston can never turn. There are 2 pins in the back of the inner pad that prevent it. The piston seal also will prevent it turning.<P>The part that runs on the adjusted screw is a separate piece inside the piston assembly. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||ka4nkf||04-28-2001||12:32 PM||||Mr ogre<BR>You are right the piston does not turn. the piston has a self adjusting nut inside the piston. Oh well I tried.<BR>My procedure will adjust the parking brake.<BR>The more I look at thes brakes The more I wish I had never seen them.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Master_Sushi||04-28-2001||03:44 PM||||I do often use my parking brake. Since I have an automatic not all the time though. I only gave the adjusting nut like 5 turns and it only moved 1/2 a cm, so I dought its overtightend. Im glad I did this though. Ive allways heard people praising the 88 brakes, now that I got mine working up to specs I can see for myself how good they really  are  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.<P>Thanks a bunch.||||reg||14
